1. Arithmetic Operations Script
Brief Description -
This script takes two numbers from the user and performs basic arithmetic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division). It also handles division by zero to prevent errors.
Key Learning Points -
•	User input with read 
•	Arithmetic with $(( )) 
•	If statements and conditionals 
•	Handling division by zero 
•	Output with echo 
Challenge Overcome -
Adding a check to prevent division by zero errors.
Why Bash Matters in DevOps -
Bash helps automate simple calculations and logic in system scripts and DevOps workflows.
 
2. Directory and File Automation Script
Brief Description -
This script creates a directory, navigates into it, creates a file, writes text including the current date, and displays the file contents.
Key Learning Points -
•	mkdir and cd usage 
•	Writing to files using > 
•	Using $(date) for dynamic values 
•	Reading files with cat 
•	Automating steps in sequence 
Challenge Overcome -
Making sure the script correctly navigates into the directory before creating the file.
Why Bash Matters in DevOps -
Bash is used to automate file and directory setup in Linux environments.
 
3. File Permission Checker Script
Brief Description -
This script checks if a file exists and displays whether it is readable, writable, and executable.
Key Learning Points -
•	File existence check -e 
•	Permissions -r, -w, -x 
•	If-else conditions 
•	User input handling 
•	Clear output formatting 
Challenge Overcome -
Managing multiple permission checks in a clean way.
Why Bash Matters in DevOps -
Bash is important for managing file permissions and system security tasks.
 
4. Backup Automation Script
Brief Description -
This script creates a timestamped backup folder, copies all .txt files from a source directory, and shows how many files were backed up.
Key Learning Points -
•	Using date for timestamps 
•	Creating directories with mkdir -p 
•	Looping with for 
•	File filtering with *.txt 
•	Counting files with variables 
Challenge Overcome -
Handling cases where no .txt files exist.
Why Bash Matters in DevOps -
Bash is widely used for backups, automation, and system maintenance tasks.
